nal stress can be explained natively, as being from a non-diphthongising dialect on the one hand, and by comparing the irregular stress shift in such words as Holunder, Wacholder, lebendig on the other. However, it seems likely that both developments were reinforced by Romance words such as Italian ermellino, armellino, French hermine. The origin of these latter is contested; they are either borrowed from Germanic or go back to an unrelated Medieval Latin Armenius mūs (literally “Armenian mouse”).",{"g":"n:m","decl":"|||s|e|e|en|e","alt":[["n","|||s|e|e|en|e"],["m","|||s|e|e|en|e"]]}]],"senses":[[18559,"hermelin","noun","ermine (Mustela erminea)",0,"n"],[18560,"hermelin","noun","ermine",1,"n"],[18561,"hermelin","noun","ermine (white fur of the ermine)",2,"m"],[18562,"hermelin","noun","A moth (Trichosea ludifica (Noctuidae spp.))",3,"m"]]},"ig.":{"entries":[["ig.",null,null,null]],"senses":[[375004,"ig.","adj","alternative form of idg. (“IE (Indo-European)”)",0]],"inflections":[["ig.","indogermanisch","alternative,abbreviation"]]},"lady":{"entries":[["lady",null,"/ˈlɛɪ̯di/","18th century, borrowed from English lady.",{"g":"f","decl":"||||s/1ies|s/1ies|s/1ies|s/1ies"}]],"senses":[[8283,"lady","noun","lady, Lady (British noblewoman)",0,"f"],[8284,"lady","noun","lady (genteel woman)",1,"f"]],"inflections":[["lady","lord","feminine"]]},"leiseres":{"entries":[["leiseres",null,null,null]],"senses":[[616547,"leiseres","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative neuter singular comparative degree of leise",0]],"inflections":[["leiseres","leise","comparative,neuter,nominative,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"liebkosend":{"entries":[["liebkosend",null,null,null]],"senses":[[655027,"liebkosend","verb","present participle of liebkosen",0]],"inflections":[["liebkosend","liebkosen","participle,present"]]},"lindert":{"entries":[["lindert",null,null,null]],"senses":[[86227,"lindert","verb","inflection of lindern:",0],[86228,"lindert","verb","third-person singular present",1],[8622